---
<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Prepositions of <em>time and place</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Choose the frame English uses for a time or place: <span class="ex">at 9:30</span>, <span class="ex">on Monday</span>, <span class="ex">in July</span>, <span class="ex">at work</span>, <span class="ex">on the table</span>, and <span class="ex">in the kitchen</span>.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">at / on / in</span>
    <span class="tag">time and place</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Read one meeting sentence.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Sort point, day, and space.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Choose and type at, on, in.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Plan a fictional community event.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">At</span>, <span class="ex">on</span>, and <span class="ex">in</span> help the listener build a frame: a point, a day or surface, or a period or space. This picture is a useful starting rule, but English also has conventional chunks such as <span class="ex">at night</span>, <span class="ex">at home</span>, and <span class="ex">on the weekend</span>.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved third-person starts, plural There are, and the zero-frequency adverb never before the main verb.">
    <h3>Three quick checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Answer from memory, then check. Say each complete sentence aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="start">start</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There is">There is</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="never">never</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="starts">starts</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There are">There are</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="always">always</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional class schedule lists a 9:00 start. My class <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="starts" data-hint="The singular subject my class takes the third-person form starts." data-fix="My class starts at nine." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> at nine. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(present simple)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The floor plan introduces two rooms: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="There are" data-hint="Two meeting rooms is plural, so introduce them with There are." data-fix="There are two meeting rooms on this floor." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> two meeting rooms on this floor. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(there is / there are)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The fictional drink log shows zero nighttime coffee. I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="never" data-hint="Zero occasions supports never before the ordinary verb drink." data-fix="The fictional speaker never drinks coffee at night." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> drink coffee at night. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(adverbs of frequency)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>One line from a work calendar</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"The meeting is on Monday at 9:30 in the small conference room."</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which small word goes with the day, <span class="ex">Monday</span>?</li>
      <li>Which small word goes with the clock time, <span class="ex">9:30</span>?</li>
      <li>Which small word goes with the room?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Clock points take at, days and surfaces take on, and months, containers, and areas take in.">
    <h3>Discovery check: choose at, on, or in</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Ask: is it a point, a day or surface, or a period or space?</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="at">at</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on">on</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="in">in</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The posted schedule gives one exact departure time. The train leaves <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="at" data-hint="An exact clock time is treated as a point, so use at." data-fix="The train leaves at 7:15." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> 7:15.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The weekly calendar gives one named day. My English class is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on" data-hint="A named day takes on." data-fix="My English class is on Tuesday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Tuesday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The fictional profile gives only the birthday month. Her birthday is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in" data-hint="A month is a longer time period, so use in." data-fix="Her birthday is in March." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> March.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The device touches the desk’s top surface. Your phone is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on" data-hint="Contact with a surface takes on." data-fix="Your phone is on the desk." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the desk.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The tickets are inside a closed bag. The tickets are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in" data-hint="Something inside a container takes in." data-fix="The tickets are in my fictional bag." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> my fictional bag.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Dana is waiting at the mapped pickup point. Dana is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="at" data-hint="A bus stop is treated as a point on the route, so use at." data-fix="Dana is at the bus stop." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the bus stop.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: time with at, on, and in</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>For time, begin with the size of the frame. The smaller and more exact the time, the more likely it is <span class="ex">at</span>. This is a thinking tool, not a complete law, so keep common expressions as whole chunks.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>preposition</th><th>use it with</th><th>examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>at</b></td><td>clock times and exact points</td><td>at 9:00, at noon, at midnight, at night</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>on</b></td><td>days and dates</td><td>on Monday, on July 4, on Friday morning, on my birthday</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>in</b></td><td>longer periods</td><td>in July, in 2026, in the summer, in the morning</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Small, medium, large</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">The review is at 9:30 on Monday in July.</span></p>
    <p>One sentence, three sizes: <span class="ex">at 9:30</span> is a point on the clock, <span class="ex">on Monday</span> is one square on the calendar, and <span class="ex">in July</span> is a whole month around it. Zoom out, and the preposition changes.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Three conventional time chunks</h3>
    <p>Learn these as chunks: <span class="ex">at night</span>, <span class="ex">on the weekend</span> in American English, and <span class="ex">in the morning / in the afternoon / in the evening</span>. Notice the pattern shift: <span class="ex">in the morning</span>, but <span class="ex">on Friday morning</span>, because the named day becomes the organizing unit.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="note-line">Use no time preposition before <span class="ex">this, next, last, every</span> or before <span class="ex">today, tonight, tomorrow, yesterday</span>: <span class="ex">this morning, next Friday, last year, every Monday, tonight, tomorrow morning</span>.</p>

  <h2>The system: place with at, on, and in</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>For place, ask what picture the sentence creates: a point you stop at, a surface or line something sits on, or a space with an inside. With activities and institutions, English often treats the location as a functional point: <span class="ex">at work</span>, <span class="ex">at school</span>, <span class="ex">at a meeting</span>. The focus is being there for the activity, not standing inside one exact room.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>preposition</th><th>use it with</th><th>examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>at</b></td><td>points, exact addresses, and activities</td><td>at work, at the bus stop, at 245 Oak Street, at the door</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>on</b></td><td>surfaces and lines</td><td>on the table, on Main Street, on the second floor, on the wall</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>in</b></td><td>enclosed spaces and areas</td><td>in the kitchen, in the city, in Mexico, in my bag</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Zoom in, and the preposition changes</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">The conference is in Mexico, in Guadalajara, at the convention center, in Room 4, at the front.</span></p>
    <p>There is no contradiction. Each phrase changes the scale of the mental map, from a large container down to a precise point.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Street line or exact address?</h3>
    <p>Use <span class="ex">on</span> for the street as a line or general location: <span class="ex">The café is on Oak Street</span>. Use <span class="ex">at</span> when an exact street number makes the location a point: <span class="ex">The café is at 245 Oak Street</span>. A person can wait <span class="ex">at the café</span> while a sign is <span class="ex">on the café door</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Transportation chunks in U.S. English</h3>
    <p>People normally travel <span class="ex">on a bus, on a train, on a plane</span>, but <span class="ex">in a car, in a taxi</span>. Treat these as conventional chunks, not a perfect physical rule. For location, say <span class="ex">I’m on the bus</span> and <span class="ex">My bag is in the car</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: point, surface, container</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The same three pictures organize both the map and the calendar. <span class="ex">At</span> marks a point, <span class="ex">on</span> marks a surface or a line (a street, a floor, a row of days), and <span class="ex">in</span> marks something with an inside (a room, a city, a month). When two prepositions are both possible, they paint different pictures.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>sentence</th><th>picture</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">She is at the hospital.</span></td><td>Her general location or purpose is the hospital.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">She is in the hospital.</span></td><td>She is inside; in American English this often means she is a patient.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">He is at the table.</span></td><td>He is beside it, sitting or working there.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">The keys are on the table.</span></td><td>They touch its surface.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Prepositions do not translate word for word. Learn combinations as chunks: <span class="ex">at work</span>, <span class="ex">on time</span>, <span class="ex">in bed</span>, <span class="ex">on the weekend</span>.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Same noun, different picture</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>contrast</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="ex">at school / in school</span></td><td><span class="ex">At school</span> gives the location or activity. In U.S. English, <span class="ex">in school</span> often means the person is a student.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="ex">in bed / on the bed</span></td><td><span class="ex">In bed</span> means resting or sleeping there. Something <span class="ex">on the bed</span> touches its surface.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="ex">on time / in time</span></td><td><span class="ex">On time</span> means at the scheduled time. <span class="ex">In time</span> means early enough before it is too late.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: keep the preposition light and the time or place clear</h3>
    <p>In neutral speech, <span class="ex">at, on,</span> and <span class="ex">in</span> are usually small linking words. The strongest beat falls on the information that follows: <span class="ex">at <strong>NINE</strong></span>, <span class="ex">on <strong>MON</strong>day</span>, <span class="ex">in <strong>JU</strong>ly</span>, and <span class="ex">in the <strong>KITCH</strong>en</span>.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>phrase</th><th>natural rhythm</th><th>main information</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>at nine</td><td>at-<b>NINE</b></td><td>the exact time</td></tr>
        <tr><td>on Monday</td><td>on-<b>MON</b>day</td><td>the day</td></tr>
        <tr><td>in the office</td><td>in-the-<b>OFF</b>ice</td><td>the location</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Do not pause after the preposition. Link it forward as one spoken group: <span class="ex">at-eight</span>, <span class="ex">on-Oak Street</span>, and <span class="ex">in-August</span>. In fast conversation, unstressed <span class="ex">at</span> often has a reduced vowel, approximately <span class="ex">uht</span>, but the following time stays clear.</p>
    <p>The preposition becomes strong when the frame itself is the contrast: <span class="ex">Meet me <strong>AT</strong> the station, not <strong>IN</strong> the station.</span> The first means the station is our meeting point. The second emphasizes being inside it.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read time, address, and transportation listening check">
    <h2>Listen: which frame did the speaker choose?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each fictional schedule or location once at a natural speed and once more slowly. Listen for the linked preposition and the complete time or place chunk. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You heard an exact time, a named day, a month, an exact address, public transportation, and a small-vehicle chunk.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Time and place listening chunks">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="at 8:30">at 8:30</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on Tuesday">on Tuesday</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="in October">in October</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="at 245 Oak Street">at 245 Oak Street</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on the bus">on the bus</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="in a taxi">in a taxi</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Clip 1 schedules the call <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="at 8:30" data-hint="Listen for the reduced point preposition linked directly to the exact clock time." data-fix="The call starts at 8:30." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Clip 2 places the appointment <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on Tuesday" data-hint="Listen for on linked to one named day." data-fix="The appointment is on Tuesday." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Clip 3 places the training period <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in October" data-hint="Listen for in linked to the month name." data-fix="The training begins in October." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Clip 4 gives the exact café location <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="at 245 Oak Street" data-hint="A numbered address is treated as one point, so listen for at before the number." data-fix="The café is at 245 Oak Street." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Clip 5 says the fictional speaker is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on the bus" data-hint="Public transportation normally uses the conventional chunk on the bus." data-fix="I’m on the bus now." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Clip 6 says the fictional visitors arrived <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in a taxi" data-hint="A small vehicle normally uses the conventional chunk in a taxi." data-fix="They arrived in a taxi." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
      <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>
    <details class="thinking-card">
      <summary>Open the teacher script after checking</summary>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="ex">The project call starts at 8:30.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">The appointment is on Tuesday, not Wednesday.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">The training begins in October.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">The café is at 245 Oak Street, on the corner.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">I’m on the bus now, so I’ll call when I arrive.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">The fictional visitors arrived in a taxi.</span></li>
      </ol>
      <p>Link each unstressed preposition to the following information. On the second reading, stress the preposition only when it corrects a different frame.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. The schedule uses point, day, day-part, and month frames, while the map uses floor, city, stop, and surface frames.">
    <h3>Drill 1: at, on, or in across your day</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Read the whole sentence first. Decide time or place, then decide the size of the picture.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="at">at</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on">on</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="in">in</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional calendar gives one exact start time. The meeting starts <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="at" data-hint="An exact clock time is treated as a point, so use at." data-fix="The meeting starts at 2:30." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> 2:30.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The work log shows zero Friday shifts. I don’t work <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on" data-hint="A repeated named day still takes on." data-fix="The fictional employee doesn’t work on Fridays." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Fridays.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The communication policy assigns email to the morning period. I answer email <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in" data-hint="The conventional day-part chunk is in the morning." data-fix="I answer email in the morning." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the morning.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The building directory lists the office on one level. Our office is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on" data-hint="English treats a floor as a level in a stack, so use on." data-fix="Our office is on the fifth floor." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the fifth floor.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The fictional profile names Mexico City as the containing city. The characters live <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in" data-hint="A city is treated as an area around the location, so use in." data-fix="The fictional characters live in Mexico City." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Mexico City.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The transit map marks one pickup point. Wait for the fictional traveler <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="at" data-hint="A bus stop is treated as a route point, so use at." data-fix="Wait for the fictional traveler at the bus stop." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the bus stop.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>The paper schedule touches the wall surface. The schedule is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on" data-hint="Something attached to a surface takes on." data-fix="The schedule is on the wall by the door." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the wall by the door.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>The project timeline gives December as the ending month. The project ends <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in" data-hint="A month is a longer time period, so use in." data-fix="The project ends in December." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> December.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-answer-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each typed preposition matches an exact time, named day, year, street line, container, or activity location.">
    <h3>Drill 2: type at, on, or in</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">No bank this time. Ask yourself: point, day or surface, or period or space? Then type the word.</p>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional shift schedule gives an exact end time. My shift ends <input class="answer-input" data-answer="at" data-hint="An exact clock time is treated as a point, so use at." data-fix="My shift ends at 6:00." aria-label="Time preposition before the exact shift end time 6:00" /> 6:00.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The calendar names both a day and its morning. The kickoff call is <input class="answer-input" data-answer="on" data-hint="A named day organizes the day part, so use on Monday morning." data-fix="The kickoff call is on Monday morning." aria-label="Time preposition before the named day Monday morning" /> Monday morning.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The fictional history gives only the move year. The characters moved there <input class="answer-input" data-answer="in" data-hint="A year is a longer time period, so use in." data-fix="The fictional characters moved there in 2023." aria-label="Time preposition before the move year 2023" /> 2023.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The map gives Main Street as the pharmacy’s general street line, not a numbered address. The pharmacy is <input class="answer-input" data-answer="on" data-hint="A general street location takes on; an exact numbered address would take at." data-fix="The pharmacy is on Main Street." aria-label="Place preposition before Main Street as a general street location" /> Main Street.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The fictional inventory places the keys inside one pocket. The keys are <input class="answer-input" data-answer="in" data-hint="Something inside a container takes in." data-fix="The keys are in the fictional coat pocket." aria-label="Place preposition before a coat pocket containing the keys" /> the fictional coat pocket.</span></div>
