  <h2>Use it in real life</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your real address, home, belongings, schedule, workplace, school, travel, family, or live location. Use a fictional map, calendar, room image, or public venue whenever you prefer.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Fictional calendar:</b> Place five events at clock times, on named days, and in months or years. Include one expression with no preposition, such as <span class="ex">next Friday</span>.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Staged room image:</b> Place five visible objects at a point, on a surface, or in a container. Explain what picture makes each preposition natural.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Map and address:</b> Put a fictional café on Oak Street and at a numbered address. Add a meeting point at the café and one sign on its door.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Transportation update:</b> Create two fictional messages using <span class="ex">on the bus / train / plane</span> and <span class="ex">in a car / taxi</span>.</div>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: plan a fictional community event</h3>
    <p>Give a 45–60 second invitation and travel update. Include all six pieces of evidence:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>one exact time with <span class="ex">at</span>, one named day with <span class="ex">on</span>, and one longer period with <span class="ex">in</span>;</li>
      <li>one point, one surface or line, and one containing area;</li>
      <li>a general street location and an exact numbered address;</li>
      <li>one public-transport or small-vehicle chunk;</li>
      <li>one expression with no time preposition, such as <span class="ex">next Saturday</span>;</li>
      <li>one spoken correction that stresses the preposition: <span class="ex">AT the station, not IN the station</span>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The listener draws the event on a calendar and map. Revise any phrase that produces a different time or place picture from the one you intended.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reflect</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Say the system back</h3>
    <p>Complete this aloud: “A point, like a clock time or a bus stop, takes ______. A day, a date, or a surface takes ______. A longer period or a space with an inside takes ______.”</p>
    <p>Then use a fictional calendar and map to say one time sentence, one place sentence, and one close contrast such as <span class="ex">on Oak Street / at 245 Oak Street</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You matched exact times, days, periods, surfaces, areas, points, and U.S. time chunks to at, on, or in.">
